 },
 "research_note": "ST. MICHAEL'S CHURCH, built by T-3689 for the 1871 scene: the German Catholic congregation's new brick church at the south-east corner of Hurlbut and Linden Streets (today Cleveland Avenue and Eugenie Street), 'a very large brick building two hundred feet long by eighty feet wide, with a tower surmounted by a low steeple containing a chime of bells', costing $200,000. Its walls stood through the fire; the steeple burned and the bells fell (Andreas vol. 2, p. 405). Read off Andreas's engraving of the ruins.",
